From d5c04604d5869d26ddb58268ab62c03f83147589 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Dave Seddon <dave.seddon.ca@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 11 Jul 2026 09:21:03 -0700
Subject: [PATCH net-next v1 0/6] net: flow_dissector: opt-in UDP-tunnel inner-flow descent

G'day,

For the UDP tunnels -- VXLAN, Geneve, GTP-U, and Tom Herbert's FOU/GUE
encapsulations -- the flow dissector stops at the outer UDP header, so
every hash-based consumer (RSS, RPS, ECMP, bonding, tc-flower, aRFS,
cake/fq fairness) sees the *outer* tunnel identity, not the inner flow.
All inner flows between a pair of tunnel endpoints collapse onto a
single hash bucket. GTP-U is the sharpest case -- no outer source-port
entropy at all, and it is the mobile-core data path where millions of
subscriber flows ride one tunnel between two endpoints.

This series adds opt-in inner descent, each tunnel behind its own
default-off gate (net.flow_dissector.{vxlan,geneve,gtpu,gue,fou}_inner):
the dissector parses through the tunnel header and dissects the inner
flow, for IPv4 and IPv6 underlays alike. Enabling a gate changes
skb->hash for that traffic -- a deliberate, per-tunnel-type operator
choice, which is why this is a separate series from the byte-identical
fast-path series it builds on:

  https://lore.kernel.org/netdev/SERIES-A-LORE-LINK/

The descent keeps that series' byte-identical contract: the slow path
grows the same gated descent (__skb_flow_dissect_udp_encap()) as the
fast path (flow_dissect_fast_udp_inner()), sharing one header-validation
classifier per tunnel (flow_dissect_{vxlan,geneve,gtpu,gue,fou}_inner_ok()),
so the fast and slow paths never disagree -- the descent subset is
defined once, not mirrored by hand. The only behaviour change is versus
today's non-descending default, and only when an operator turns a gate
on. The KUnit suite dissects each tunnelled packet through the fast path
and through the slow path -- with and without the STOP_AT_FLOW_LABEL
flag skb_get_hash() passes -- and memcmp()s the result (patch 5).

With every gate off, the added slow-path cost is one not-taken static
branch per UDP packet: the per-tunnel gates sit behind a single summary
key (flow_dissector_udp_inner_any_key) maintained by their sysctl
handler, so the count of tunnel types does not grow the hot path.

The descents validate the tunnel header before trusting it: VXLAN
requires a plain RFC 7348 header (I flag set, all other flag bits zero
-- GBP/GPE defer), Geneve requires version 0, no options and no OAM,
GTP-U requires a v1 G-PDU with no option flags. Callers passing
FLOW_DISSECTOR_F_STOP_{AT,BEFORE}_ENCAP (RPS/RFS, tunnel drivers) keep
the outer tuple even with a gate on. tc-flower tunnel matching
(FLOW_DISSECTOR_KEY_ENC_*) is unaffected -- those keys come from decap
tunnel metadata via skb_flow_dissect_tunnel_info(), not from this
descent. Descent recursion shares the fast-path series'
MAX_FLOW_DISSECT_HDRS bound; a crafted overlay-in-overlay chain stops
at the same depth on both paths (covered by a nested-VXLAN KUnit case).

FOU and GUE (patch 4) are Tom Herbert's UDP encapsulations. Unlike
VXLAN/Geneve/GTP-U they have no well-known UDP port, so a packet cannot
be recognised from the wire alone; the dissector consults the per-netns
FOU table, reached from the built-in dissector through a small
registered ops hook (the fou module is tristate; registration failure
logs a pr_warn). GUE is self-describing; direct FOU carries its inner
protocol in that table rather than on the wire.

Port recognition is by IANA destination port (4789/6081/2152) plus the
header validation above; FOU/GUE go through the fou table instead. A
non-tunnel UDP packet sent to a tunnel port that happens to validate is
hashed by its would-be inner flow -- inherent to on-wire tunnel
recognition, and one reason the gates are opt-in: an operator enables
one for deployments where that port is the tunnel by policy.

CPU for the descents is ~net-zero (the inner work cancels the outer
fast-path saving); the value is the inner-flow hash
distribution/identity.

The descent gates are deliberately excluded from the auto-enable
controller proposed in a separate RFC thread: enabling them changes
hashing and must stay a deliberate operator action.

Testing: in-tree KUnit descent equivalence (patch 5; both underlay
families, out-of-subset negatives, STOP-flag contracts, nested-chain
bound; run under KASAN+UBSAN), a CONFIG_NET_FOU {n,m,y} x
CONFIG_FLOW_DISSECTOR_KUNIT_TEST config matrix (with fou built in, the
real module owns the ops hook and the FOU/GUE cases skip cleanly),
per-patch W=1, sparse and smatch clean. Supporting data lives in the
author's XDP2 fork: https://github.com/randomizedcoder/xdp2 , under
perf-results/ .

On tooling: as with the fast-path series, developed with substantial
LLM assistance (Claude, Anthropic), human-reviewed and human-measured;
every patch carries an Assisted-by: trailer in the
Documentation/process/coding-assistants.rst format.

